        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "When the blood glucose concentration decreases <b>below</b> the set point, a hormone is released from the pancreas.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 21
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Describe how the release of this hormone leads to the blood glucose concentration returning to the set point.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "14.1(9)",
            "role": "primary"
          },
          {
            "ref": "14.1(10)",
            "role": "secondary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 7,
          "instruction": "<i>any <b>seven</b> from:</i>",
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "glucagon <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "binds to receptor on cell surface membrane (of liver cell) <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "G-protein activated <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "adenyl(yl) cyclase activated <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "ATP converted to cAMP <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 6,
              "text": "second messenger <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 7,
              "text": "enzyme cascade / described <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 8,
              "text": "enzymes activated by phosphorylation <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 9,
              "text": "signal amplified <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 10,
              "text": "glycogen broken down into glucose / glycogenolysis <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 11,
              "text": "glucose enters blood <b>;</b>"
            },
            {
              "n": 12,
              "text": "gluconeogenesis / described <b>;</b>"
            }
          ],
          "source": {
            "ms": 13
          }
        },
        "er": [
          "er.q6.b"
        ],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "The hormone is glucagon, and it acts on liver cells by cell signalling. Glucagon binds to a receptor on the cell surface membrane, which activates a G-protein, which activates adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP into cAMP, the second messenger.",
              "refs": [
                "ms:b#1",
                "ms:b#2",
                "ms:b#3",
                "ms:b#4",
                "ms:b#5",
                "ms:b#6",
                "syllabus:14.1(9)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "cAMP starts an enzyme cascade in which enzymes are activated by phosphorylation, so the signal is amplified. The final enzyme breaks glycogen down to glucose; gluconeogenesis also makes glucose, and the glucose leaves the liver cells into the blood, raising the concentration back to the set point.",
              "refs": [
                "ms:b#7",
                "ms:b#8",
                "ms:b#9",
                "ms:b#10",
                "ms:b#11",
                "ms:b#12",
                "syllabus:14.1(10)"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Name the hormone: glucagon.",
            "State where it binds: a receptor on the cell surface membrane of liver cells.",
            "Follow the signalling sequence: G-protein, adenylyl cyclase, ATP to cAMP (second messenger).",
            "Describe the cascade: enzymes activated by phosphorylation, amplifying the signal.",
            "Finish with the outcome: glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, then glucose entering the blood."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Stating that glucagon enters the liver cell, rather than binding to a receptor on the cell surface membrane.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:b#2"
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Jumping from the hormone straight to glycogen breakdown and missing the signalling steps that carry most of the marks.",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "ms:b#3",
                "ms:b#4",
                "ms:b#5",
                "ms:b#7"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      }
